        "math_academy_id": "q-182221",
        "problem": "If $A={0}$ and $B={5,6}$ then $|P(P(A))\\times B|=$",
        "worked_solution": "For any set $S,$ we have $|P(S)|={2}^{|S|}.$ Also, $|S\\times T|=|S|\\cdot |T|.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n|A|=1,\\,|B|=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(A)| & ={2}^{|A|}={2}^{1}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(P(A))| & ={2}^{|P(A)|} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|P(P(A))\\times B| & =|P(P(A))|\\cdot |B| \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
